---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import ModelWorkbench from '../../../components/tutorials/ModelWorkbench';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Mass-Spring-Damper"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Mass-Spring-Damper' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Your First Model', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/first-model' }}
  next={{ label: 'Bouncing Ball', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/bouncing-ball' }}
>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The mass-spring-damper is the canonical introductory ODE system. A mass <em>m</em> is attached to a spring
    with stiffness <em>k</em> and a damper with coefficient <em>c</em>. The governing equations are:
  </p>
  <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-sm text-text-muted mb-6">
    dx/dt = v<br/>
    m &middot; dv/dt = -k &middot; x - c &middot; v
  </div>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    In Modelica, this is expressed naturally. Try compiling the model below with different backends.
  </p>

  <ModelWorkbench
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model MassSpringDamper
  Real x(start = 1);
  Real v(start = 0);
  parameter Real k = 1, c = 0.1, m = 1;
equation
  der(x) = v;
  m * der(v) = -k * x - c * v;
end MassSpringDamper;`}
    modelName="MassSpringDamper"
    tEnd={10}
    defaultScript={`ctx.onInit = (api) => {
  const { THREE, state } = api;
  state.scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x101010);
  const light = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.0);
  light.position.set(3, 4, 2);
  state.scene.add(light);
  state.scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0x404040, 0.9));
  state.scene.add(new THREE.GridHelper(12, 24, 0x2f4f63, 0x2a2a2a));
  // Wall anchor
  const wall = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.2, 1.5, 1.5),
    new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x666666 })
  );
  wall.position.set(-2, 0.75, 0);
  state.scene.add(wall);
  // Mass block
  state.mass = new THREE.Mesh(
    new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.5, 0.5, 0.5),
    new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xf0732e })
  );
  state.mass.position.set(0, 0.25, 0);
  state.scene.add(state.mass);
  // Spring line
  state.spring = new THREE.Line(
    new THREE.BufferGeometry(),
    new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({ color: 0x15b7e7 })
  );
  state.scene.add(state.spring);
};
ctx.onFrame = (api) => {
  const x = api.getValue("x", api.sampleIndex);
  const pos = Number.isFinite(x) ? x : 0;
  if (api.state.mass) api.state.mass.position.x = pos;
  if (api.state.spring) {
    const pts = [];
    const segs = 20;
    const amp = 0.15;
    for (let i = 0; i <= segs; i++) {
      const t = i / segs;
      const sx = -1.9 + t * (pos + 0.25 - (-1.9));
      const sy = 0.25 + (i > 0 && i < segs ? amp * Math.sin(i * Math.PI) * (i % 2 ? 1 : -1) : 0);
      pts.push(new api.THREE.Vector3(sx, sy, 0));
    }
    api.state.spring.geometry.dispose();
    api.state.spring.geometry = new api.THREE.BufferGeometry().setFromPoints(pts);
  }
};`}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">Understanding the Output</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
    In the CasADi output, <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">x</code> is the state vector
    containing position and velocity,
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">p</code> contains the parameters (k, c, m), and the ODE function
    maps (x, p) &rarr; dx/dt. You can use this directly with CasADi integrators or optimal control solvers.
  </p>
</TutorialLayout>
